/*!
@addtogroup ewdg
@brief Instance-based driver layer for the External Watchdog Monitor.

@details
The EWDG driver wraps the low-level register helpers and exposes the public
`EWDG_DRV_*` API set used by applications. The driver validates startup
parameters, protects the two-key refresh sequence from interrupt latency, and
provides lightweight helpers for input-mode query and interrupt masking.

@section ewdg_driver_features Key Features
- Validates the requested refresh window before enabling the watchdog.
- Resolves the peripheral base address internally from the instance index.
- Masks global interrupts around refresh and restore sequences.
- Exposes a default-configuration helper for `ewdg_init_config_t`.
- Provides a readback helper for the configured `EWDG_in` assert behavior.
- Supports optional interrupt-driven safety handling while leaving IRQ setup to
  the application.

@section ewdg_driver_modes Supported Workflows

| Workflow | APIs | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| Default startup | EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() + EWDG_DRV_Init() | Build a valid startup configuration and enable the watchdog. |
| Periodic servicing | EWDG_DRV_Refresh() | Service the watchdog inside the valid refresh window. |
| Input-behavior query | EWDG_DRV_GetInputPinAssertLogic() | Read back the external input-pin mode encoded in `CTRL`. |
| Interrupt masking | EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt() | Switch from interrupt-assisted handling to polling or logging only. |
| Reset-like restore | EWDG_DRV_DeInit() | Restore the register block to reset-like values. |

@section ewdg_driver_arch Driver Architecture

The public driver layer sits between application code and the inline hardware
access helpers:

@verbatim
+-----------------------------------------------+
|               Application Code                |
|          (calls EWDG_DRV_* functions)         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|         EWDG Driver Layer (ewdg_driver.c)     |
|  - Window validation                          |
|  - Instance-to-base resolution                |
|  - Refresh / restore interrupt protection     |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|       EWDG HW Access Layer (ewdg_hw_access.h) |
|  - Raw register programming helpers           |
|  - Refresh-key write helper                   |
|  - Reset-like restore helper                  |
+-----------------------------------------------+
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_driver_arch_init Startup Flow
EWDG_DRV_Init() first verifies that the module is not already enabled and that
the requested compare window is valid. It then programs the prescaler, optional
clock source, compare registers, and finally writes the control-register image
that enables the watchdog.

@subsection ewdg_driver_arch_service Refresh Flow
EWDG_DRV_Refresh() masks global interrupts, calls EWDG_Refresh(), and then
restores the global interrupt state so the two-key service sequence is not
interrupted.

@section ewdg_driver_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection ewdg_driver_config_struct ewdg_init_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Default From EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() |
|-------|------|-------------|------------------------------------------|
| clockSource | ewdg_clock_source_t | Watchdog clock source when the field is present. | EWDG_SIRC_CLOCK |
| assertLogic | ewdg_in_assert_logic_t | External input-pin assert behavior. | EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ZERO |
| interruptEnable | bool | Enable watchdog interrupt generation. | false |
| prescaler | uint8_t | Prescaler field written to `CLK_CTRL`. | 255U |
| compareLow | uint16_t | Earliest accepted refresh count. | FEATURE_EWDG_CMPL_MIN_VALUE |
| compareHigh | uint16_t | Latest accepted refresh count. | FEATURE_EWDG_CMPH_MAX_VALUE |

@subsection ewdg_driver_config_input Input Assert Modes
| Value | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_DISABLED | Do not use `EWDG_in` in the watchdog decision path. |
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ZERO | Assert when `EWDG_in` is low. |
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ONE | Assert when `EWDG_in` is high. |

@section ewdg_driver_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection ewdg_driver_usage_basic Pattern 1: Default Driver Startup
Start the watchdog with the default configuration and service it in the main
loop.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

ewdg_init_config_t ewdgConfig;

EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&ewdgConfig);
if (EWDG_DRV_Init(INST_EWDG, &ewdgConfig) == STATUS_SUCCESS)
{
    while (1)
    {
        EWDG_DRV_Refresh(INST_EWDG);
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection ewdg_driver_usage_irq Pattern 2: Install an Interrupt Handler Before Startup
When interrupt-assisted handling is required, install the shared watchdog IRQ
handler before enabling the module.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

void EWDG_Watchdog_ISR(void)
{
    /* Log the event or trigger a controlled safety action here. */
    EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt(INST_EWDG);
}

void EWDG_InitWithInterrupt(void)
{
    ewdg_init_config_t ewdgConfig;

    INT_SYS_InstallHandler(WDOG_EWDG_IRQn, &EWDG_Watchdog_ISR, (isr_t *)0);
    INT_SYS_EnableIRQ(WDOG_EWDG_IRQn);

    EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&ewdgConfig);
    ewdgConfig.interruptEnable = true;
    (void)EWDG_DRV_Init(INST_EWDG, &ewdgConfig);
}
@endcode

@subsection ewdg_driver_usage_query Pattern 3: Read Back the Input Mode
Use the driver helper to convert the raw control bits into the public enum.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

ewdg_in_assert_logic_t inputMode;

inputMode = EWDG_DRV_GetInputPinAssertLogic(INST_EWDG);
if (inputMode == EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ONE)
{
    /* The external input asserts the safety path when it goes high. */
}
@endcode

@section ewdg_driver_api API Quick Reference

@subsection ewdg_driver_api_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_Init() | Validate the startup configuration and enable the watchdog. |
| EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() | Populate an `ewdg_init_config_t` structure with default values. |
| EWDG_DRV_DeInit() | Restore the register block to reset-like values. |

@subsection ewdg_driver_api_service Watchdog Service
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_Refresh() | Perform the protected two-key refresh sequence. |

@subsection ewdg_driver_api_ctrl Status & Interrupt Control
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_GetInputPinAssertLogic() | Decode the current `EWDG_in` behavior. |
| EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt() | Clear the watchdog interrupt-enable bit. |

@section ewdg_driver_notes Important Notes

@subsection ewdg_driver_notes_clock Clocks and Pins Are External Responsibilities
The driver assumes the EWDG peripheral clock and external watchdog pins have
already been configured. It does not call the clock manager or the PORT driver.

@subsection ewdg_driver_notes_once Startup Is Intended to Happen Once Per Reset
If the hardware already reports the EWDG as enabled, EWDG_DRV_Init() returns
`STATUS_ERROR` and leaves the instance unchanged.

@subsection ewdg_driver_notes_window Refresh Timing Must Match the Programmed Window
The driver only validates the compare values during startup. It does not read
the running counter before each refresh, so the application must schedule
EWDG_DRV_Refresh() correctly.

@subsection ewdg_driver_notes_irq Shared Interrupt Routing
The EWDG interrupt shares the watchdog interrupt vector on supported devices.
The application must install and enable the IRQ handler explicitly.

@subsection ewdg_driver_notes_mask Interrupt Masking Scope
EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt() only clears the interrupt-enable bit. It does not
deinitialize the module, change the input-pin mode, or clear external safety
outputs.

@section ewdg_driver_integration Integration Guide

@subsection ewdg_driver_integration_units Compilation Units
Compile the following file into the application:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ewdg/ewdg_driver.c
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_driver_integration_include Include Path
Add the driver include directory to the toolchain include path:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_driver_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile symbols are required beyond the active device selection.

@subsection ewdg_driver_integration_deps Dependencies
The public driver depends on `device_registers.h`, `status.h`, and
`interrupt_manager.h`. The inline hardware access layer is pulled in through
`ewdg_hw_access.h`.
*/
